    {
      "id": "the-merry-wives-of-windsor/sceneIV_23.6/p@158859",
      "type": "speech",
      "tag": "p",
      "classes": [
        "drama"
      ],
      "text": "HOST.\nI will hear you, Master Fenton, and I will, at the least, keep your counsel.",
      "separatorAfter": "\n\n",
      "spans": [],
      "lines": [
        {
          "id": "the-merry-wives-of-windsor/sceneIV_23.6/p@158859/line-1",
          "start": 0,
          "end": 5,
          "sourceLineNumber": null
        },
        {
          "id": "the-merry-wives-of-windsor/sceneIV_23.6/p@158859/line-2",
          "start": 6,
          "end": 82,
          "sourceLineNumber": null
        }
      ],
      "sourceRef": {
        "start": 4202321,
        "end": 4202433,
        "line": 155664,
        "column": 1,
        "path": "chap23/p@158859",
        "anchor": "sceneIV_23.6"
      },
      "speakerLabel": "HOST.",
      "attribution": {
        "status": "explicit",
        "cueStart": 0,
        "cueEnd": 5
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "the-merry-wives-of-windsor/sceneIV_23.6/p@158975",
      "type": "speech",
      "tag": "p",
      "classes": [
        "drama"
      ],
      "text": "FENTON.\nFrom time to time I have acquainted you\nWith the dear love I bear to fair Anne Page,\nWho mutually hath answered my affection,\nSo far forth as herself might be her chooser,\nEven to my wish. I have a letter from her\nOf such contents as you will wonder at,\nThe mirth whereof so larded with my matter\nThat neither singly can be manifested\nWithout the show of both, wherein fat Falstaff\nHath a great scene; the image of the jest\nI’ll show you here at large. Hark, good mine host:\nTonight at Herne’s oak, just ’twixt twelve and one,\nMust my sweet Nan present the Fairy Queen—\nThe purpose why is here—in which disguise,\nWhile other jests are something rank on foot,\nHer father hath commanded her to slip\nAway with Slender, and with him at Eton\nImmediately to marry. She hath consented. Now, sir,\nHer mother, even strong against that match\nAnd firm for Doctor Caius, hath appointed\nThat he shall likewise shuffle her away,\nWhile other sports are tasking of their minds,\nAnd at the dean’ry, where a priest attends,\nStraight marry her. To this her mother’s plot\nShe, seemingly obedient, likewise hath\nMade promise to the doctor. Now thus it rests:\nHer father means she shall be all in white\nAnd in that habit, when Slender sees his time\nTo take her by the hand and bid her go,\nShe shall go with him. Her mother hath intended\nThe better to denote her to the doctor,\nFor they must all be masked and vizarded—\nThat quaint in green she shall be loose enrobed,\nWith ribbons pendant flaring ’bout her head;\nAnd when the doctor spies his vantage ripe,\nTo pinch her by the hand, and on that token\nThe maid hath given consent to go with him.",
      "separatorAfter": "\n\n",
